# Design: recall-bench comparative harness

## Problem
ohya claims that a shared SQL/FTS memory beats grepping raw transcript
archives, but nothing measured the claim with real clients. The existing
memory-e2e gate checks recall correctness against a seeded isolated hub;
it cannot compare access paths or cost.

## Approach
Two arms run the same headless Claude Code client over the same question
body; only the access preamble and tool allowlist differ (raw: Bash,
Grep, Read, Glob over the three transcript roots; ohya: mcp__ohya__sql
over the production hub db, isolated through --strict-mcp-config and a
scratch CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR so no auto-memory or user MCP leaks in).
Ground truth is mechanical: answerContains strings plus an evidence
citation that the harness resolves (file path containing the marker for
the raw arm; events.id whose session carries the marker for the ohya
arm — session granularity, because the two formats cannot share record
granularity fairly). A preflight verifies every marker's hub-session,
settled-session, and local-file counts before any timed run.

## Key Trade-offs
- Canaries planted through the real CLIs keep ground truth exact and
  test the live pipeline, but curation of real-history questions echoes
  markers into the curator's own session (queries, tool results, even
  writing the question file), so curated expected counts must include
  the curation session; expected_settled exists because that session is
  live during the bench.
- Per-message usage records are zeroed by the API proxy in use, so the
  parser prefers the result event's cumulative usage.
- ToolSearch is allow-listed in the cross-access audit: it carries no
  transcript data and fires when the client defers tool schemas.
- Token totals measure context economy, which is the mechanism under
  test: grep over multi-megabyte JSONL lines versus bounded SQL rows.

